{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791458156,"nonce":"vkmhqvgs/ZGL/Xf339cJpnrjtR8=","title":"HIR: defer immutable temporary allocations until a consuming escape","message":"Lua op_overload still allocates two 72-byte objects per iteration after typed operator inlining. The heap scalarizer correctly rejects the pointer webs: generic metamethod fallback results join allocations in phis, and boxing can expose their identity.\n\nImplement guarded scalar fields alongside a backing pointer, preserving actual loads for opaque incoming objects. Allocate and initialize a virtual object only when an opaque call observes it. Require a consuming escape: no tracked pointer or alias is live after the call, and reject writes through joined pointers, overlapping fields, unsupported pointer operations, and simultaneous distinct virtual arguments. Keep all Lua dispatch guards and fallback semantics.\n\nValidate HIR and executable behavior for loops, dynamic incoming objects, identity/alias retention, mutation after escape, null paths, and rejected live aliases. Compare frozen binaries on ARM64 and the NUC with LLVM off and interpreter-first execution; record short and long wall/kernel time and check that no builds run during timing.\n\nBaseline commit: 7bc52ae96b622db080d3407c6b36ecaf0feb82de. Existing escape checks are necessary; do not loosen them without materialization and lifetime proof.","files":null}]}